::ALG2_U02_L06_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>If x represents a length, which value is not viable? {
=x = -4 #Correct. Lengths cannot be negative.
~x = 0 #Zero may be possible depending on the context.
~x = 3 #Positive lengths can be viable.
~x = 12 #Positive lengths can be viable.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>In h(t) = -16t(t - 5), what does t = 5 usually represent? {
~initial height #Initial height is h(0).
~maximum height #Maximum height occurs at the vertex.
=landing time #Correct. The later zero is when height returns to 0.
~starting time #Starting time is t = 0.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>What should be checked after solving a polynomial model? {
~only the leading coefficient #The leading coefficient is not enough.
=whether each solution is reasonable in context #Correct. Context decides viable solutions.
~only the degree #The degree is not enough.
~only the constant term #The constant term is not enough.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>If P(x) models profit, what does P(x) = 0 represent? {
~maximum loss #A maximum loss would be a minimum value.
~domain restriction #A domain restriction limits inputs.
~negative profit #Negative profit is below zero.
=break-even point #Correct. Profit of 0 is break-even.
}

::ALG2_U02_L06_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U02_L06_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Why might a complex solution be rejected in a physical length model? {
~complex solutions are always wrong #Complex solutions may be valid algebraically.
=physical lengths must be real numbers #Correct. Physical lengths are real-valued measurements.
~all negative values are complex #Negative real values are not complex.
~models never have complex values #Some equations can have complex solutions.
}

